# Move-order chains under chaining: a monoid.
#
# Sources (read, not modified):
#   ontology-pflow-xyz ROADMAP.md:420-423 @f854116 (Phase 6, "What the categorical
#     reading actually is"): "Orderers: a monoid under chaining (the empty chain []
#     is the identity, concatenation is associative, and the composite is
#     lexicographic). It is a *partial* monoid in code: chains over 8 pieces and
#     repeated pieces are refused, and DECISIVE pieces form a top tier. The law
#     that matters: every composite keeps ct:order-only."
#   pflow-rs crates/pflow-metamodel/src/order.rs @c396d46:
#     1-7    a chain of pieces, each a sort key per move, compared one after the
#            other; "Order never changes a value, only how soon a cutoff comes"
#     13     MAX_PIECES = 8
#     117-123  the five pieces: tt_best, immediate_win, history, centrality, threats
#     205-216  Chain::default() = [tt_best, immediate_win, history]
#     220-230  Chain::new: refuses more than 8 pieces, keeps the list as given
#     250-265  key(): one slot per piece, stop after a decisive nonzero key;
#              before(): the key arrays compared lexicographically
#   pflow-rs crates/pflow-metamodel/src/plan.rs @c396d46:
#     8        the doc plan's chain ["tt_best", "immediate_win", "centrality", "history"]
#     196-210  SearchPlan::chain(): absent "order" is Chain::default(); a repeated
#              piece is refused (204-205); then Chain::new
#
# Reading. A wire of type Order carries an order chain. A piece is a state
# None -> Order (the one-piece chain [p]). chain : Order * Order -> Order is
# concatenation (left operand first = higher priority); empty is the chain [],
# which plan.rs gets from "order": [] (Chain::new(&[]); every key array is empty,
# before() says Equal, ties keep the net's order). An ABSENT "order" is not []:
# it is Chain::default(), defined below as default_order.
#
# Why these are the laws. order.rs builds one flat key array over the chain's
# pieces in list order (250-259) and compares arrays lexicographically
# (262-265). Concatenating [a,b] with [c] or [a] with [b,c] gives the same list,
# hence the same array and the same comparator: associativity holds on the nose.
# [] contributes no slots: it is a unit on both sides. That is the content of
# the three ASSUMED rules below; everything after them is PROVED by Chyp.
#
# Typing does the ROADMAP's "law that matters": every generator here has output
# type Order (ct:order-only, ROADMAP.md:372), and chain maps Order * Order to
# Order, so every composite term is again an Order. That is closure of a type,
# checked by Chyp's type checker when a term is built, not a rewrite.
#
# NOT equational, so not in this theory (all of these are side conditions the
# checker cannot see; this file is the free monoid on the five pieces):
#   - repeats: plan.rs:204-205 refuses ["history", "history"]. Chain::new itself
#     accepts repeats (order.rs test a_chain_has_a_limit, 288-292, builds eight
#     history pieces), so the refusal lives in SearchPlan, not in Chain.
#   - the cap: Chain::new refuses more than 8 pieces (order.rs:221-226). With
#     repeats refused and only five pieces, no JSON plan can reach the cap; it
#     binds only callers of Chain::new.
#   - the top tier: a decisive piece (tt_best, immediate_win: order.rs:52-66)
#     with a nonzero key zeroes the slots after it (order.rs:254-256). That is a
#     data-dependent property of the comparator, not an equation between chains.
#   - commutativity: [tt_best, history] and [history, tt_best] are different
#     chains (Chain derives PartialEq on its Vec) and can try moves in a different
#     order, so node counts can differ. They give the same VALUE (order.rs:4-6); that
#     is the separate rule order_only below, stated on the value, not the chain.

gen tt_best : None -> Order "f2d7a0"
gen immediate_win : None -> Order "f2d7a0"
gen history : None -> Order "f2d7a0"
gen centrality : None -> Order "f2d7a0"
gen threats : None -> Order "f2d7a0"

gen chain : Order * Order -> Order "ffffff"
gen empty : None -> Order "ffffff"

# ASSUMED (order.rs:250-265 read as list concatenation; ROADMAP.md:420-421)
rule assoc : chain * id ; chain = id * chain ; chain
rule unitL : empty * id ; chain = id[Order]
rule unitR : id * empty ; chain = id[Order]

# ASSUMED (order.rs:205-216): the chain an absent "order" means.
def default_order = tt_best * immediate_win * history ; chain * id ; chain

# The plan.rs:8 doc chain, built left-nested (as a parser folding the JSON list
# from the left would) and right-nested (as a cons-list would).
let doc_left  = tt_best * immediate_win * centrality * history ; chain * id * id ; chain * id ; chain
let doc_right = tt_best * immediate_win * centrality * history ; id * id * chain ; id * chain ; chain

# PROVED: the two bracketings are the same chain. Each step is one application
# of assoc, found and checked by Chyp's rule tactic.
rewrite doc_chain_bracketing :
  doc_left
  = tt_best * immediate_win * centrality * history ; chain * chain ; chain by assoc
  = doc_right by assoc

# PROVED: the balanced bracketing ([tt_best, immediate_win] ++ [centrality, history])
# is the same chain too; simp with the monoid laws reaches the unique normal form.
rewrite doc_chain_balanced :
  tt_best * immediate_win * centrality * history ; chain * chain ; chain
  = doc_right by simp(assoc, unitL, unitR)

# PROVED: appending the empty chain changes nothing ("order": [..., ] ++ []).
rewrite doc_chain_append_empty :
  doc_left * empty ; chain
  = doc_left by unitR

# PROVED: prepending it changes nothing either.
rewrite doc_chain_prepend_empty :
  empty * doc_right ; chain
  = doc_right by unitL

# PROVED: the default chain followed by [] is the default chain (unfolding the
# def, then unitR, then folding it back).
rewrite default_append_empty :
  default_order * empty ; chain
  = tt_best * immediate_win * history * empty ; chain * id * id ; chain * id ; chain by default_order_def
  = tt_best * immediate_win * history ; chain * id ; chain by unitR
  = default_order by -default_order_def

# The value. value : Order -> Value is the solver's value of one fixed
# position of one fixed net, run with the chain on its input.
gen value : Order -> Value "dddddd"

# ASSUMED (order.rs:4-6, "alpha-beta returns the same number whatever order it
# tries the moves in"; ROADMAP.md:372 ct:order-only): the value does not see
# the order of the two halves of a chain. The code's claim is stronger (the
# value does not depend on the chain at all); this weaker rule is enough to
# show that commutativity holds after value and not before.
rule order_only : sw ; chain ; value = chain ; value

# PROVED: the value is the same for [tt_best, history] and [history, tt_best].
rewrite value_ignores_order :
  tt_best * history ; chain ; value
  = history * tt_best ; chain ; value by -order_only

# PROVED: the doc chain and the doc chain with its halves exchanged
# ([centrality, history] ++ [tt_best, immediate_win]) give the same value.
rewrite value_ignores_halves :
  tt_best * immediate_win * centrality * history ; chain * chain ; chain ; value
  = centrality * history * tt_best * immediate_win ; chain * chain ; chain ; value by -order_only

# NEGATIVE CONTROL (must fail): the chains themselves do not commute. The
# monoid laws give unique normal forms (Chyp README, "Tactics"), and these two
# are distinct normal forms, so this is not derivable here, which is right.
rewrite NOT_chains_commute :
  tt_best * history ; chain
  = history * tt_best ; chain by simp(assoc, unitL, unitR)
